---
import LearnLayout from "../../components/learn/LearnLayout.astro";
import LearnNav from "../../components/learn/LearnNav.astro";
import { tagBlob, RELEASE_URL, RELEASE_CHANGELOG } from "../../data/learn";

const description =
  "How hipfire 0.4.1 reuses prefill work on Flash-Next, a hybrid model with recurrent layers: an engine-owned session cache of copy-plus-delta snapshots at chunk boundaries, cold-exact across sessions, plus live continuation inside a conversation.";
---

<LearnLayout title="Session cache on a hybrid model" description={description}>
  <article class="distil">
    <div class="distil-meta"><a href="/learn">/learn</a> &middot; prompt reuse</div>
    <h1 class="distil-title">Session cache on a hybrid model</h1>
    <p class="distil-lede">
      Agents resend almost the same prompt every turn: the same system prompt,
      the same tool list, a conversation that only grew at the end. Prefilling
      all of it again is the slowest thing an engine can do on a long context.
      0.4.1 gives Flash-Next an <strong>engine-owned session cache</strong> that
      keeps many prompts warm at once, and <strong>live continuation</strong>
      for the next turn of the same conversation.
    </p>
    <p class="distil-lede">
      The cache is built on <a href="https://github.com/warpfront/hipfire/pull/825">#825</a>
      and <a href="https://github.com/warpfront/hipfire/pull/826">#826</a> by
      <strong>@fivetide</strong>, which introduced the engine-owned session cache
      and the message-end snapshot work.
    </p>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">01</span> Why prefix caching is harder on a hybrid model</h2>
      <span class="lx-tag">the idea</span>
      <p>
        In a plain transformer, the state after <em>n</em> tokens is just the
        first <em>n</em> rows of the KV cache. To reuse a shared prefix you keep
        those rows and stop there; any cut point works.
      </p>
      <p>
        Flash-Next is hybrid. Three of every four layers are Gated DeltaNet
        layers, which keep a fixed-size recurrent state that summarizes the
        <em>whole</em> prefix; only the remaining layers (QSA attention) keep
        per-token rows. The recurrent state after token <em>n</em> cannot be
        recovered from the state after token <em>m &gt; n</em>. So you can only
        resume where you saved the state, and you have to decide where to save
        it before you know which prefix the next request will share.
      </p>
      <div class="distil-fig">
        <div class="fig-caption">fig &middot; what a resumable point has to contain</div>
        <pre class="ascii-fig">{`  fixed-size state (whole, every time)     append-only rows (grow with the prefix)
  ───────────────────────────────────     ───────────────────────────────────────
  Gated DeltaNet recurrent + conv state    QSA K/V rows
  per-layer n-gram embedding (PLE) state   raw and pooled index keys
  hyper-connection state                   the MTP head's own rows (with MTP)
  current QSA selections
  MTP head state + draft policy (with MTP)`}</pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">02</span> Snapshots at chunk boundaries: copy plus delta</h2>
      <span class="lx-tag">how hipfire does it</span>
      <p>
        Prefill already runs in chunks (8,192 rows on Strix Halo, 4,096 on the
        R9700). Every time a prefill crosses a chunk multiple, the engine takes
        a snapshot there. A snapshot holds a <strong>copy</strong> of the
        fixed-size state, and only the <strong>delta</strong> of the
        append-only rows: the rows above its parent, which is the deepest
        snapshot of the same prefix.
      </p>
      <div class="distil-fig">
        <div class="fig-caption">fig &middot; two sessions sharing a system prompt</div>
        <pre class="ascii-fig">{`  session A:  [ chunk 0 ][ chunk 1 ][ chunk 2 ][ tail ]
  session B:  [ chunk 0 ][ different text …            ]

  cache:      S0  = state@c0 + rows[0, c0)          ← shared root
               ├─ S1  = state@c1 + rows[c0, c1)
               │   └─ S2 = state@c2 + rows[c1, c2)  ← A restores S0 → S1 → S2, prefills its tail
               └─ (B's own snapshots at its boundaries)

  restore = walk the chain from the root; every link adds one chunk of rows`}</pre>
      </div>
      <p>
        Because every link carries one chunk of rows, a snapshot costs about the
        same at any depth, and sessions that share a prefix (sub-agents with one
        system prompt, say) share its links. On Strix Halo each Flash-Next
        snapshot is 513&nbsp;MiB at any depth; full copies would grow by
        481&nbsp;MiB per chunk, so a 64K-token session needs about 4&nbsp;GiB for
        all eight boundaries instead of about 17&nbsp;GiB.
      </p>
      <ul class="distil-ul">
        <li><strong>Commit-gated.</strong> A snapshot is published only after the client commits the turn, and never depends on live state, so a reset does not drop it.</li>
        <li><strong>LRU over leaves.</strong> A snapshot with children is pinned; eviction removes only leaves.</li>
        <li><strong>Budget.</strong> Default 8&nbsp;GiB (<code>memory.session_cache_bytes</code>); copies live in system RAM on unified memory and in VRAM on discrete cards, behind a free-memory guard.</li>
        <li><strong>Engine-owned.</strong> <code>hipfire_runtime::session_cache</code> owns keying, planning, eviction, the memory guard, placement and every copy; the model only describes its state parts and prefill boundaries.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">03</span> Cold-exact and session-exact</h2>
      <span class="lx-tag">the idea</span>
      <p>
        There are two different promises a cache can make, and 0.4.1 keeps them
        apart:
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th></th><th>cross-session snapshot</th><th>live continuation</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>when</td><td>any request whose prompt shares a cached prefix</td><td>the next turn of the same conversation, if its tokens strictly extend what the previous turn consumed</td></tr>
            <tr><td>what runs</td><td>restore the deepest snapshot, prefill the rest</td><td>prefill only the new suffix, in place, no copy</td></tr>
            <tr><td>exactness</td><td><strong>cold-exact</strong>: equals a cold prefill of the same prompt</td><td><strong>session-exact</strong>: the state descends from decode, as in one continuous conversation</td></tr>
            <tr><td>captures</td><td>yes, at chunk boundaries</td><td>no; the shared cache only ever holds cold-exact state</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        When the live state and the longest cached snapshot reach equally far,
        the live state wins, and a hit always prefills a non-empty suffix. The
        hardware test restores a three-link chain and a shared root on both the
        AR and native-MTP routes and checks every state part, the final logits
        row and the MTP seed byte-for-byte against a cold prefill. AR and MTP
        snapshots never cross routes.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">04</span> What it buys you</h2>
      <div class="lx-buys">
        <div class="lx-buys-head">0.4.1 serve gates &middot; Radeon AI PRO R9700 &middot; Flash-Next</div>
        <div class="lx-stats two">
          <div class="lx-stat">
            <div class="lx-stat-val accent">68–78<span class="unit">%</span></div>
            <div class="lx-stat-label">of prompt tokens served from cache</div>
            <div class="lx-stat-sub">plain chat conversations and cross-session prefixes, MTP on and off</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lx-stat">
            <div class="lx-stat-val">0<span class="unit">errors</span></div>
            <div class="lx-stat-label">request errors across all 24 serve-gate runs</div>
            <div class="lx-stat-sub">a 64K-token prompt at max_seq 262,144 on the VMM backend decodes coherently</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="lx-buys-foot">
          The cache replaces the earlier single prefix checkpoint, which kept one
          prompt only: the next request with a different prefix (another session,
          a sub-agent) overwrote it.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">05</span> Limits and switches</h2>
      <div class="lx-limit">
        <div class="lx-limit-title">known issues in 0.4.1</div>
        <ul class="distil-ul">
          <li><strong>Tool-call conversations do not reuse the cache.</strong> A turn whose history contains earlier tool calls is re-rendered differently from the committed record, so it is not a strict extension and gets no live continuation; TC-shaped cross-session traffic got no hits either (0 of 13 turns on the R9700). Task-eval numbers in the release therefore carry no cache speedup.</li>
          <li>One request executes at a time per loaded model; concurrent HTTP requests queue.</li>
          <li>Message-end snapshots (restore at the deepest <code>&lt;|im_end|&gt;</code> instead of a chunk boundary, fivetide's idea) exist but are off by default (<code>HIPFIRE_QWEN4_TURN_SNAPSHOTS=1</code>) until gated.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <p>
        <code>HIPFIRE_SESSION_CACHE_BYTES=0</code> turns the cache and live
        continuation off together; every turn then prefills cold. The previous
        prefix and radix caches for Flash-Next are removed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">06</span> Sources</h2>
      <ul class="distil-ul">
        <li><a href={tagBlob("docs/ARCHITECTURE.md")}><code>docs/ARCHITECTURE.md</code></a> &sect; &ldquo;Qwen4 session cache and live continuation&rdquo;.</li>
        <li><a href={tagBlob("crates/hipfire-runtime/src/session_cache.rs")}><code>crates/hipfire-runtime/src/session_cache.rs</code></a>.</li>
        <li><a href={RELEASE_CHANGELOG}>CHANGELOG, v0.4.1</a> (session cache, live continuation, message-end snapshots) and the <a href={RELEASE_URL}>v0.4.1 release notes</a> (feature modes, R9700 serve gates, known issues).</li>
        <li>@fivetide, <a href="https://github.com/warpfront/hipfire/pull/825">#825</a> / <a href="https://github.com/warpfront/hipfire/pull/826">#826</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <LearnNav current="/learn/session-cache" />
  </article>
</LearnLayout>
